### 2.8.6 Experimental Results

We evaluated the proposed method through three complementary comparisons: matching the target reference, comparing results with flagship cameras, and testing additional real-world captures without paired references.

**1. Target Reference Matching**

Figure 1 compares results from six indoor scenes featuring portraits, warm or mixed lighting, bright backgrounds, and dim interiors. Each row shows the original 16-bit linear RGB input without tone mapping, the output of the model trained on iPhone-paired data, our enhanced result, the S26 camera output, and the iPhone reference.

Our method improves the visibility of both subjects and backgrounds, with overall brightness, color, and facial detail broadly comparable to the reference. Compared with the baseline and S26 outputs, the enhanced results show more natural-looking skin tones and better-balanced local contrast. The differences are less noticeable under even lighting. Scenes with strong brightness differences or mixed illumination remain challenging, with insufficient shadow enhancement or residual color casts in some cases.

**2. Comparison with Flagship Cameras**

Figure 2 compares our method with the iPhone 17 Pro Max, Huawei Pura 90, vivo X300U, and S26U in an extreme low-light group portrait under strong colored lighting. Results from the June 2026 milestone build and the current build are also included.

The current build makes foreground subjects clearly visible while preserving facial detail and background structure. Skin tones appear relatively natural, but subject brightness remains moderate. Compared with the iPhone and Huawei outputs, the subjects stand out less from the background, giving the image a somewhat flat appearance. This indicates a need for stronger subject separation through local tone and contrast adjustment.

**3. Real-World Evaluation without Paired References**

Figure 3 compares the baseline with the model fine-tuned using regenerated training targets on two additional real-world scenes: a portrait against bright signage and a group portrait under strong colored lighting.

Both models produce clearly visible images from linear inputs that appear nearly black without tone mapping. Compared with the baseline, the fine-tuned model preserves more visible detail in bright areas and produces more natural-looking skin tones and a better-balanced brightness distribution, while maintaining similar shadow visibility. These examples suggest that regenerated training targets can improve rendering on additional real-world captures.

Together, the three comparisons show that the method can approach the target rendering, maintain useful detail in challenging scenes, and benefit from regenerated training targets. However, the findings are qualitative. Cross-device differences in acquisition and processing, together with the limited test set, prevent broader conclusions about generalization or the contribution of individual network components.

### 2.8.7 Limitations

**1. Shadow–Highlight Balance and Subject Separation**

Balancing shadow enhancement with highlight preservation remains difficult in high-contrast scenes. Under extreme low light, foreground subjects are visible but do not stand out as strongly as in some flagship camera outputs, resulting in relatively flat rendering.

**2. Evaluation and Generated-Target Reliability**

The current evaluation is based on a limited number of qualitative comparisons. Additional testing is needed to assess generalization and isolate the contribution of individual components. Regenerated training targets may also introduce unwanted changes in image content, texture, or color, making careful selection and validation important.

### 2.8.8 Conclusion and Future Work

The proposed two-stage tone mapping network converts high-bit-depth linear RGB inputs into display-ready sRGB images. Qualitative results show improved subject visibility and overall rendering broadly comparable to the target references. Fine-tuning with regenerated training targets also improves visible highlight detail, skin appearance, and brightness balance in the additional real-world examples.

The main remaining challenges are balancing shadow and highlight rendering, improving foreground–background separation, and maintaining consistent color under mixed illumination.

Future work will focus on region-aware tone and contrast adjustment, particularly for faces, dark subjects, and bright backgrounds. We will also refine the selection of regenerated training targets to improve rendering consistency while preserving scene content. Evaluation will be expanded to held-out scenes, controlled ablation studies, and region-level measurements of brightness, color, and detail. Finally, runtime performance will be measured on the target hardware to assess deployment feasibility.
